{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"An ETF’s price stays close to its underlying value mainly through **arbitrage and the creation/redemption mechanism**.\n\n- **At a premium:** Authorized participants—typically large financial institutions—can buy the underlying asset basket, exchange it for newly created ETF shares, and sell those shares. This puts downward pressure on the ETF’s price.\n- **At a discount:** They can buy ETF shares, redeem them for the underlying assets, and sell those assets. This helps push the ETF’s price upward.\n\nMarket makers’ competing buy and sell quotes also help maintain alignment.\n\n**Worth knowing:** This isn’t a guarantee. Transaction costs, illiquid holdings, market stress, or closed underlying markets can allow premiums or discounts to persist. Published net asset value (NAV) is usually calculated daily; trading prices reflect changing intraday expectations."}}
